@flexiarg institutions/minimal-recognition-of-rights-to-organise
@title Minimal Recognition Of Rights To Organise
@keywords autonomy, external authority, recognition, Ostrom 7, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 7, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/recognised-right-to-self-organise]

! conclusion:
  "The rights of appropriators to devise their own institutions are not challenged by external governmental authorities."

  + context:
    User-made institutions sit within states and other external authorities.

  + IF:
    Users have devised their own rules for a resource.

  + HOWEVER:
    External authorities have legitimate interests, and some local institutions are unjust or ineffective.

  + THEN:
    Secure at least minimal recognition from external authorities that the users may devise and enforce their own institutions.

  + BECAUSE:
    If outside authorities can override or ignore local rules at will, users have little reason to invest in making or keeping them.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/recognised-right-to-self-organise.]
